The speaker immediately defines the practical content of that line. To think and then say what we are thinking, while still sounding coherent in the act of saying it, is a skillset: $\begin{aligned}
\text{thinking} &\Longrightarrow \text{speaking coherently},\\
\text{skill} &\Longrightarrow \text{trainability}.\end{aligned}$ That second line is the lecture’s first important move. We are not being told that fluency belongs only to the naturally gifted. We are being told that if the mechanism is a skill, then it can be trained. The entire lecture rests on that reclassification.

